---
description: The theme grid option was given something other than a Theming API theme object or the string 'legacy'.
---

## Invalid Theme Grid Option

The `theme` grid option accepts a Theming API theme object, or the string `legacy`. It was given {% errorParam name="theme" fallback="ag-theme-alpine" /%}, which is neither, so the grid cannot apply it.

Where the value looks like a CSS class name, such as `ag-theme-alpine` or `ag-theme-quartz`, the cause is almost always a legacy theme name being passed to the option. In v32 and earlier a theme was chosen by putting that class on the grid's container element. The `theme` option introduced in v33 is a different mechanism and does not take those names.

### Use the matching theme object

Theme objects are imported from `ag-grid-community` and passed directly. `themeQuartz`, `themeAlpine`, `themeBalham` and `themeMaterial` correspond to the legacy themes of the same name:

```js
import { themeAlpine } from 'ag-grid-community';
```

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    theme: themeAlpine,
}
```

The object can be customised before use, for example `themeAlpine.withParams({ accentColor: 'red' })`. See [Theming API](./theming/).

### Or keep the legacy themes

To stay on the v32 approach, pass the string `legacy` and keep applying the theme class to the container element as before. See [Continue with Legacy Themes](./theming-migration/#continue-with-legacy-themes).

Where you have several grids, a [Global Grid Option](./grid-interface/#global-grid-options) sets this once for all of them:

```ts
import { provideGlobalGridOptions } from 'ag-grid-community';

// Mark all grids as using legacy themes
provideGlobalGridOptions({ theme: 'legacy' });
```

{% note %}
If you also see [error #239](./errors/239) or [error #106](./errors/106), the legacy CSS files are still loaded alongside a Theming API theme. Those pages cover removing them.
{% /note %}
